WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.760
How many mistakes can a movie have before
it just implodes?

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.360
With the help of Movie Mistakes, We're here
to find out.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:11.560
From major blockbusters to holiday classics,
these movies were all littered with ridiculous

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:13.960
mistakes, but all found their audience just
the same.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.320
The moment Johnny Depp – dread-locked and
eyeliner-ed – stepped smoothly off the mast

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.660
of his sinking ship and onto the dock of Port
Royal, the world changed.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:25.200
Pirate movies had been considered a dead genre
for a long time.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:29.420
The Country Bears, another movie based off
a Disney park ride, had flopped only a year

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:30.100
earlier.

00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:34.460
But what once had been considered for direct-to-video
release ended up as one of the highest-grossing

00:00:34.460 --> 00:00:35.880
films of 2003.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.660
Pirates were back, in big, brash, box office-blowout
fashion, and suddenly, a movie based on a

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.920
Disney ride was making mad stacks.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.240
But a reviewing of the famous first film of
the trilogy reveals a cavalcade of mistakes.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:51.480
Visible crew members and equipment litter
the movie, like the man in a tan cowboy hat

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.900
and a white t-shirt standing behind Jack Sparrow
as he commands his crew:

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.200
"On deck, you scabrous dogs"

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.559
Then there's the imprints of very modern sneakers
visible in the foreground of a shot of a dusty

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:02.680
workshop floor.

00:01:02.680 --> 00:01:06.480
Moreover, the movie's grasp of the 18th century
is tenuous at best.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.560
Soldiers shoot multiple times with guns that
were invented nearly a century later.

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.759
Captain Norrington is promoted to commodore,
despite the fact that "commodore" was a posting

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:15.680
and not a rank.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:19.640
Pirates might've blown audiences away with
its verve and humor, but it sure didn't win

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:21.280
any awards for precision.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:26.619
The archetypal disaster movie, Twister dazzled
audiences with its up-close portrayal of nature's

00:01:26.620 --> 00:01:28.860
destructive streak and the people who chronicle
it.

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.560
Tornadoes don't just rip apart the landscape
in Twister — they suck people into their

00:01:32.570 --> 00:01:37.540
murky vortices, grow more than a mile wide,
and skewer bystanders with flying shards of

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:38.140
metal.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:42.900
By the end of the movie, tornadoes seem less
like acts of god and more like gods themselves.

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.300
They're life-changingly powerful and impossible
to understand, let alone control.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.020
Natural disasters really do upend lives and
landscapes, of course.

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.920
But Twister played fast and loose with the
actual science of tornadoes in order to ramp

00:01:54.920 --> 00:01:56.020
up the spectacle.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:00.700
The tornado that opens the movie is described
as an "F5" according to the Fujita Scale...

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.080
which didn't exist in the year the scene is
set.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:07.860
Later, when a burgeoning tornado is described
by its Fujita ranking, Twister ignores the

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.920
fact that the rating can't be determined until
the tornado is gone.

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.120
But the climax of the film takes the cake.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.640
Our heroes send a car hurtling into a tornado
and book it through a cornfield at full tilt,

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.940
without being delayed by the sturdy stalks.

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:24.560
The belts they use to lash themselves to pipes
manage to keep them safe, and relatively unscathed,

00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:27.100
while the tornado passes directly over them.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:29.200
The storm, it seems, has a sense of drama.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.640
It sends some cows hurtling through the air,
but it leaves the leads alone.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.960
Nothing is subtle in the world of The Phantom
of the Opera.

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.900
Beds are shaped like enormous swans.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:40.000
Candles are strewn everywhere by the hundred.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.920
Velvet is the fabric of choice.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:45.020
This isn't a movie interested in doing anything
by halves.

00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:48.840
Unfortunately, that happens to be the case
with the film's missteps as well.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:53.300
Some are accidentally hilarious, as when Raoul
is trapped in a chamber of water with a slowly

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:58.060
lowering grating, and then bubbles suddenly
float downwards — a dead giveaway that the

00:02:58.060 --> 00:02:59.020
film was reversed.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.960
Some are simply confusing, like Meg Giry's
pants.

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:06.480
When she enters the Phantom's lair, she wades
through water, yet one shot later, she's completely

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:07.620
bone-dry.

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.100
But the most noticeable mistake of all has
to do with the Phantom's deformity.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.220
No, we're not talking about the fact that
the movie downgraded the horrifying malformation

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:18.360
of the book and stage musical to something
like a bad sunburn.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:21.740
We're talking about the fact that it changes
over the course of its reveal.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:26.840
Before Christine rips the mask off, the disfigurement
doesn't extend much beyond his jawline, only

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:30.920
for CG to be added moments later, taking it
to his ear, neck, and beyond.

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:35.320
Moreover, while the Phantom's deformity is
luridly pink when first seen, it's almost

00:03:35.320 --> 00:03:37.160
sallow when glimpsed later in his lair.

00:03:37.160 --> 00:03:40.300
For being the thing that doomed him to a life
in the Parisian sewers, the

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.220
Phantom's terrible face sure is subject to
change.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.140
In the age of the omnipresent superhero, The
Dark Knight still stands as a high-water mark

00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:48.940
of the genre.

00:03:48.940 --> 00:03:53.560
When the film hit theaters back in 2008, Batman
fans and newbies alike thrilled to the growl

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:58.000
of Christian Bale's voice, Heath Ledger's
grimy vision of chaos, and a sleek and modern

00:03:58.000 --> 00:03:59.840
Gothan City crippled by violence.

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:02.640
"You've changed things, forever."

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.200
"Then why do you want to kill me?"

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.680
"I don't want to kill you."

00:04:08.900 --> 00:04:12.760
It was exciting, honestly groundbreaking,
and one of the most celebrated movies of the

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:13.600
2000s.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:17.920
Yet there's no level of acclaim that can keep
audiences from noticing all the little continuity

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:19.860
flubs that litter The Dark Knight.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:24.340
Props and actors have a habit of changing
position between shots, like when the Joker

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:28.290
is interrogated, his hands switch back and
forth between gripping Batman's arms from

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:29.420
above and below.

00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:33.380
The cracks in the wall of the interrogation
room shrink and expand, from covering the

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.240
surface to barely indenting it.

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:39.460
During the thrilling car chase, the Tumbler
loses a tire when it drives across a concrete

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:43.160
barrier, but then it's mysteriously reattached
between shots.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.700
The movie is so packed with action that it's
easy to miss these odd missteps, but return

00:04:47.710 --> 00:04:51.610
to the steely streets of Gotham City often
enough and even the most ardent Bat-fan will

00:04:51.610 --> 00:04:52.780
be able to pick them out.

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.520
Directed by Martin Scorsese,
the 2002 epic Gangs of New York chronicled

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.120
the world of 19th-century Five Points, a New
York City neighborhood as vibrant as it was

00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:02.920
deadly.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:07.600
Gangs vied for power, people fought and died,
and the winds of change never stopped blowing,

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:11.710
bringing immigrants, freed slaves, pickpockets,
and all other manner of misfits and dreamers

00:05:11.710 --> 00:05:12.880
into the mix.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:18.020
Led by Leonardo DiCaprio and the ever-electrifying
Daniel Day-Lewis, the film garnered positive

00:05:18.020 --> 00:05:20.060
reviews and ten Oscar nominations.

00:05:20.060 --> 00:05:23.160
Another Scorsese classic, another American
fable.

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:27.580
Like any historical drama, however, Gangs
of New York managed to foul up quite a lot

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:28.720
of its details.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:32.860
Bill the Butcher might growl about who will
do dirty deeds for a nickel, but he's missing

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:36.540
the fact that he was living at least three
years before the coining of the first nickel.

00:05:36.540 --> 00:05:40.660
A man is taken down in a theater, bananas
that weren't yet traded in the US surrounding

00:05:40.660 --> 00:05:41.160
him.

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:46.480
Flags featuring 50 stars drape a stage, roughly
a century before the US would, in fact, have

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:47.200
50 states.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:51.780
This might have been a story about an era
long past, but its mistakes were utterly modern.

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.520
The flexible nature of magic in the world
of Harry Potter allowed filmmakers a large

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:57.660
degree of freedom.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.780
Apparition allowed instant travel.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:02.300
Souls could be fractured, implanted, and linked.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:07.020
Everything from fire to bubbles could be produced
by the magic wands every major character carried

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:07.960
on their person.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:12.100
It was big, it was wild, and its possibilities
were thoroughly explored.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.680
Time travel, however, makes fools of even
the most dedicated director.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.180
Harry Potter and the Prisoner of Azkaban features
it heavily, and the movie is accordingly pocked

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.940
with mistakes and confusingly divergent shots,
lines, and scenery.

00:06:23.940 --> 00:06:28.260
When Hermione punches Malfoy, for example,
she's first seen turning away from him before

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.120
she swings.

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:33.280
Yet when Harry and Hermione watch her do it
after going back in time, she faces him head-on

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:36.420
from the very beginning and throws a markedly
different punch.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:40.980
This is to say nothing of how confusing it
is that, immediately after turning back time,

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:45.320
Hermione claims it to be 7:30, despite the
fact that a bell is audibly ringing out that

00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:46.880
it's, in fact, 7:00.

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:48.500
"7:30.

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:50.220
Where were we at 7:30?"

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:54.300
Most frustratingly, when Hermione throws a
stone through Hagrid's window, it's first

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.530
seen breaking a vase in half... it completely
shatters when the moment is revisited.

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:02.900
You might be able to fit time travel into
the plot, but you can't guarantee that everything

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:04.800
makes sense the second time around.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:08.360
Is there any movie that says "Hollywood" more
than Titanic?

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:12.480
From its legendary profits to the fact that,
more than 20 years later, people are still

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.680
making jokes about it.

00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:15.720
"Jack, I want you to draw me like one of your

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:16.580
French girls."

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:19.920
It was as big, bold, and glitzy as the movie
industry itself.

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:24.240
It's impossible to imagine the world without
Titanic, so huge was its impact.

00:07:24.240 --> 00:07:29.120
It wasn't just an icon of the 1990s, love
stories, or any featured actor's career — it's

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:30.720
just an icon, period.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:34.800
Yet Titanic is as riddled with mistakes as
any other movie in this video.

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:40.000
A Rose living in 1912 comments on Sigmund
Freud's theories on "the male preoccupation

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:44.820
with size," despite the fact that Freud wouldn't
publish material on that subject until 1920.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:45.880
"Freud, who is he?

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:46.820
A passenger?"

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:51.780
Jack runs to catch the ship, while his friend
sports a modern Swedish Army rucksack, commonly

00:07:51.780 --> 00:07:56.040
available in Army surplus stores today and
very much unavailable in his own time.

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:00.240
The "unsinkable" Margaret Brown, played to
perfection by Kathy Bates, refers to herself

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:05.080
as Molly, a nickname the actual woman never
used until after the sinking of the Titanic.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:09.680
So it goes in the world of blockbuster films,
where sweeping audiences away requires the

00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:10.840
work of thousands.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:15.040
As a result, sometimes you make a hit the
likes of which the world has seldom seen,

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:17.060
but you still probably get a few things wrong.

00:08:17.700 --> 00:08:22.400
For many, the Christmas season isn't complete
without a screening or two of the 1983 classic,

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:23.640
A Christmas Story.

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:28.820
Ralphie's pink bunny suit is childhood mortification
personified, the leg lamp can be purchased

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.930
from a variety of retailers, and even those
who've never heard of comic strip hero Red

00:08:32.930 --> 00:08:35.360
Ryder could likely tell you of his famous
BB gun.

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.920
"I want an Official Red Ryder Carbine-Action

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:39.520
Two-Hundred-Shot Range Model Air Rifle!"

00:08:41.420 --> 00:08:43.220
"You'll shoot your eye out, kid."

00:08:43.440 --> 00:08:47.140
But once one hits their hundredth screening
of the holiday tale, it becomes hard not to

00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:49.720
notice its cut corners and misplaced scenery.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:53.720
The Parker home is subject to teleporting
objects that it might as well be in the world

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:54.780
of Harry Potter.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:58.580
A Christmas bow appears and disappears from
a lampshade over the course of the scene in

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.320
which the dogs steal the turkey.

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:04.940
The leg lamp, when first lifted from the box,
has no light fixture on which to place a shade,

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.940
then gains one when it comes time to light
it.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:11.119
And then, a jar of pickles sits on the kitchen
table before a scene in which Ralphie's mom

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:14.060
comes up from the basement with the jar, placing
it on the table.

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:15.060
Time travel?

00:09:15.080 --> 00:09:16.040
Teleportation?

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:17.140
Outright magic?

00:09:17.240 --> 00:09:21.720
Nope, just the nature of filmmaking, in which
props get switched out, bows fall off, and

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.300
no one can remember when a jar was retrieved
from where.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:29.360
Two other Spider-Men have inhabited the legendary
blue and red suit since Tobey Maguire brought

00:09:29.360 --> 00:09:34.579
the webslinger to cinematic life, but Spider-Man
2 still stands as a joyous monument of superhero

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:35.200
cinema.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:36.180
The action!

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:37.000
The romance!

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:37.800
The tragedy!

00:09:37.800 --> 00:09:41.999
And what a cast, bringing it all to life,
styled to embody their comic counterparts

00:09:42.000 --> 00:09:43.760
with almost surreal perfection.

00:09:43.760 --> 00:09:48.579
Mad science drives a man insane, altruism
brings a young man with astonishing powers

00:09:48.579 --> 00:09:52.819
to the brink of death, and a moral emerges
from the chaos, as shining as the city Spider-Man

00:09:52.820 --> 00:09:56.800
protects: Doing good is the duty of all who
are capable.

00:09:56.860 --> 00:09:59.980
Something less shining, however, is what the
movie gets wrong.

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:04.180
Remember the scene when Otto Octavius' fusion
experiment goes horribly awry?

00:10:04.180 --> 00:10:08.699
Octavius warns the onlookers that what they're
about to see is dangerous and then arms himself

00:10:08.700 --> 00:10:09.700
with special goggles.

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:15.320
"Ladies and gentlemen, fasten your seatbelts."

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:19.360
Yet mere feet away, the onlookers are apparently
totally okay without them.

00:10:19.360 --> 00:10:23.220
And how about tritium, the substance
that's so rare that Octavius claims there

00:10:23.220 --> 00:10:25.340
are only 25 pounds of it on Earth?

00:10:25.340 --> 00:10:29.820
He's not entirely wrong, as tritium is, in
fact, quite rarely found in nature.

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:34.280
But it can be produced through man-made nuclear
reactions of the sort a world-famous scientist

00:10:34.280 --> 00:10:37.000
like Octavius would doubtlessly have access
to.

00:10:37.000 --> 00:10:42.660
So goes the ways of Hollywood super-science
— ever-changing, always fantastic, and extraordinarily

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:43.740
easy to screw up.

